# Preregistration

Predictions written before any of the seven new groups of order 24 was trained.
Outcomes appended after the run, unedited above the line.

---

## Design

There are exactly fifteen groups of order 24 up to isomorphism. `groups24.py`
builds all fifteen and certifies the enumeration by exhibiting fifteen distinct
isomorphism fingerprints. Eight had been trained in earlier work; seven had not:

| group | q-mass | quaternionic | trained before |
|---|---|---|---|
| Z12×Z2 | 0 | no | no |
| Z6×Z2×Z2 | 0 | no | no |
| Z2×D12 | 0 | no | no |
| Z4×S3 | 0 | no | no |
| Z3⋊D8 | 0 | no | no |
| **Z2×Dic12** | **1/3** | **yes** | **no** |
| **Z3⋊Z8** | **1/6** | **yes** | **no** |

Using the whole population removes any discretion over which groups to include.
Two of the seven unseen groups carry quaternionic-type irreducible
representations, and a third character-table twin pair appears:

| pair | shared | q-mass | tested before |
|---|---|---|---|
| D8×Z3 / Q8×Z3 | character table, k = 15, d_max = 2 | 0 vs 1/6 | yes |
| D24 / Dic24 | character table, k = 9, d_max = 2 | 0 vs 1/2 | yes |
| **Z2×D12 / Z2×Dic12** | **character table, k = 12, d_max = 2** | **0 vs 1/3** | **no** |

q-mass now takes four values (0, 1/6, 1/3, 1/2) rather than three, so it can be
tested as a dose rather than a binary.

## Conditions

ρ = 0.60, 9000 epochs, width 512, AdamW lr 2×10⁻³, β = (0.9, 0.98), weight decay
1.0, full batch, cross-entropy, one hidden layer, no biases, concatenated
one-hot inputs. Twelve instances per group; each instance draws an independent
random relabelling of the group, an independent split seed and an independent
initialisation seed.

Primary metric: mean final validation accuracy per group, with the group as the
unit of replication. Secondary: T_gen at sustained validation ≥ 0.90 over five
consecutive evaluations on the checkpoint grid.

Primary test: exact one-sided ordering probability that the five
quaternionic-type groups occupy the bottom five of fifteen, 1/C(15,5) = 3.3×10⁻⁴.
Secondary: permutation test on group means.

## Predictions

**A1 (ordering).** The five quaternionic-type groups occupy the bottom five
positions of fifteen by mean final validation accuracy at ρ = 0.60.
*Fails if* any non-quaternionic group ranks below any quaternionic one. Report
the ranking and do not quote an exact ordering p.

**A2 (new groups).** Both new quaternionic groups rank below all ten
non-quaternionic groups.
*Fails if* either Z2×Dic12 or Z3⋊Z8 ranks above any q-mass-0 group.

**A3 (third twin pair).** Z2×Dic12 has lower mean final validation accuracy than
Z2×D12. Since the two share a character table, a reversal is the cleanest
possible refutation of the design.

**A4 (dose, risky).** Mean final validation accuracy is monotone decreasing in
q-mass across {0, 1/6, 1/3, 1/2}.
*Fails if* not monotone. Expected to fail: SL(2,3) and Q8×Z3 share q-mass = 1/6
but differed by 0.15–0.20 in earlier work, so q-mass already does not set the
size of the effect. A failure sharpens the claim from "q-mass predicts sample
complexity" to "the presence of a quaternionic sector predicts its sign and
q-mass does not scale it".

**A5 (alternatives, risky).** In an out-of-sample predictive comparison, q-mass
beats every competing Cayley-table invariant — involution count, exponent,
centre order, abelianisation, class count, largest irreducible dimension,
commuting probability, element-order profile — at leave-one-group-out prediction
of mean final validation accuracy.
*Fails if* any single alternative matches or beats it. Involution count is the
live threat: it is anti-correlated with quaternionic type on the twin pairs by
construction. If it wins, the honest claim becomes "a power-map statistic
predicts sample complexity, and Frobenius–Schur type is one such statistic among
several".

**B1 (probe protocol).** Applying the probe protocol of Chou et al.
(arXiv:2605.27078, Appendix A.1.3 — AdamW, lr 10⁻³, weight decay 1.0, 200
full-batch epochs) to a frozen Q8×Z3 representation yields probe minus native
below +0.05, while closed-form ridge with λ selected inside the training split
on the same representation yields above +0.15.
*Fails if* the two agree. Then the gap is a property of the network after all
and the methodological claim is withdrawn.

**B2 (weight decay).** In the λ_train × λ_readout factorial, probe minus native
at λ_train = 0 is at least 0.10 smaller than at λ_train = 1.0.
*Fails if* the gap persists undiminished at λ_train = 0. Then training weight
decay is not implicated and §4.3 of the write-up must be retitled.

**B3 (logit scale, risky).** The gap is not explained by the effective logit
scale alone. Regressing the gap on the realised logit scale and then adding
λ_readout raises R² by more than 0.10.
*Fails if* the gap collapses onto the logit scale the way Truong
(arXiv:2606.18465) shows the grokking delay does. A failure means the readout
defect belongs to that literature rather than this one.

**B4 (spuriousness, defensive).** The representation-degradation signature of
Chou et al. — probe accuracy falling while effective rank rises — is absent in
Q8×Z3 at ρ = 0.70, wd = 1.0, and the sub-optimal-readout signature is present.
*Fails if* degradation is present. Then the regime is one Chou et al. would
classify as spurious and §4 must be re-run at a recipe that does not degrade the
representation.

**B5 (no trace-8, no defect).** Neither D24/Dic24 nor Z2×D12/Z2×Dic12 has a
trace-8 sector; every sector is trace 1 or 4. No single-sector transplant in
either pair reproduces the trace-8 repair seen in Q8×Z3.

## Not predicted

The identity of the interfering sector in any new group. Q8×Z3 repairs via the
trace-8 complex-pair sector and SL(2,3) via a different one, so no rule exists.
Any post-hoc sector identification in a new group is exploratory.

## Analysis decisions fixed in advance

The group is the unit of replication; seeds and instances are not independent
observations of the hypothesis. No group is dropped after training for any
reason. Right-censored runs are reported as censored, not imputed. The
validation split is read once per configuration, and every hyperparameter is
chosen on data held out inside the training split. If A1 fails, no exact
ordering p is quoted and the claim is downgraded to the group-level tests.

---

# Outcomes

Recorded after `e2_full_population.py`, `e1_wd_factorial.py` and
`e3_probe_protocol.py` were rerun on the corrected trainer (AdamW ordering,
loss rescaling, exact epoch count) and the corrected probe protocol (every
readout fitted on the full training split). Nothing above this line was edited.

**A1 — confirmed.** The five quaternionic-type groups occupy the bottom five
positions with no inversion: SL(2,3) 0.075, Dic24 0.198, Q8xZ3 0.271,
Z3sdZ8 0.339, Z2xDic12 0.674, then Z2xA4 0.685, S4 0.699, D8xZ3 0.739,
Z3sdD8 0.776, Z4xS3 0.804, Z24 0.995, Z6xZ2xZ2 0.996, Z12xZ2 0.998, D24 0.999,
Z2xD12 0.999. Rank sum 15, the minimum attainable. Exact one-sided ordering
test p = 1/C(15,5) = 3.3e-4. The secondary permutation test on group means gives
p = 3.3e-4 with a difference of +0.558.

Two qualifications. Eight of the fifteen groups generated the hypothesis, so
this is a preregistered extension to the complete population, not a wholly
prospective confirmation. And the boundary margin is 0.011, roughly a third of
the within-group standard deviation there.

**A2 — confirmed.** Both new quaternionic groups rank below all ten
non-quaternionic groups: Z3sdZ8 at 0.339 and Z2xDic12 at 0.674, against a
minimum non-quaternionic mean of 0.685.

**A3 — direction confirmed, premise wrong.** Z2xD12 0.999 against Z2xDic12
0.674, delta +0.325, Welch t = 35.7. The pair had never been trained and the
direction was predicted correctly.

However, **this pair is not a character-table twin.** The check used when the
preregistration was written compared only the multiset of irreducible degrees
and the multiset of conjugacy-class sizes, which are necessary but not
sufficient. Z2xDic12 has abelianisation Z2 x Z4 and hence four linear characters
taking values +-i; every linear character of Z2xD12 is real, so the tables cannot
agree under any permutation. There are exactly two character-table twin pairs at
order 24 — D8xZ3/Q8xZ3 and D24/Dic24 — and both are among the groups that
generated the hypothesis. A3 therefore stands as a preregistered degree- and
class-size-matched contrast, not as a twin replication, and no three-pair sign
test is reported. `groups24.same_character_table` now computes the actual
character table.

**A4 — failed, as anticipated.** Mean accuracy by q-mass: 0.869 (q = 0), 0.228
(1/6), 0.674 (1/3), 0.198 (1/2). Correlation -0.697, not monotone. q-mass
predicts the sign of the effect, not its size.

**A5 — not yet run.** `groups24.invariants` computes the competing invariants;
the leave-one-group-out comparison is outstanding. Involution count remains the
live threat.

**B1 — confirmed, with a smaller effect than the earlier flawed run.** On one
frozen Q8xZ3 representation the Chou recipe gives -0.021 at 200 epochs and
+0.002 at that weight decay run to convergence, while ridge with lambda chosen
inside the training split gives +0.235. Ten recipes span 0.256. The cleanest
contrast holds objective, optimiser, budget and initialisation fixed and varies
only the readout weight decay: +0.002 at 1.0 against +0.181 at 0.01. At the Chou
setting the twins are indistinguishable (+0.002 against +0.000); under ridge
they separate (+0.235 against -0.033).

An earlier run reported -0.139 for the Chou row. That run fitted the probe on
only 322 of the 403 training examples while the native readout saw all 403, so
the gap was partly a data-quantity artefact. Every recipe is now fitted on the
full training split.

**B2 — the prediction is met but the gate was badly designed.** The gap is
+0.148 at lambda_train = 0 and +0.262 at lambda_train = 1.0. The comparison is
not meaningful: at lambda_train = 0 the probe itself reaches only 0.162, so
there is no formed solution to fail to express. probe - native is not comparable
across lambda_train because the probe ceiling moves; native and probe are
reported as separate columns from here on.

What the factorial does show: the span across lambda_train at
lambda_readout = 0 is 0.865, the span across lambda_readout at lambda_train = 1
is 0.146. Training weight decay is associated with building the linearly
decodable solution; readout weight decay limits its expression. The two roles
are opposite in sign and were previously conflated.

**B3 — not yet run.** The logit scale is logged at every checkpoint; the
collapse regression against Truong (arXiv:2606.18465) is outstanding.

**B4 — confirmed.** Probe accuracy rises from near zero to 0.994-1.000 in every
run and effective rank rises with it (57-60 to 69-71), so the degradation
signature is absent in all six runs. The sub-optimal-readout signature is
present in 3/3 Q8xZ3 seeds and 1/3 D8xZ3 seeds, so it is rarer and much smaller
in the grokking twin rather than cleanly absent.

**B5 — not yet run.** Requires transplants in the D24/Dic24 pair.

**Unregistered, exploratory.** Under a donor fixed in advance, the trace-4
Frobenius-Schur-typed sector carries 60.8% sufficiency and 44.6% necessity of
the repair in Q8xZ3, against 50.0% and 37.8% for the trace-8 complex pair, with
a group-equivariant rank-8 control at 0.0% and 4.1%. Three seeds; the difference
between the two sectors is within seed noise and neither is claimed to dominate.
In D8xZ3 the whole repair range is 0.031, too small for the fractions to carry
meaning.
